I ran into an issue where I have two models that should be able to refer to each other without enforcing foreign key constraints.  This is the best solution I could come up with but I'm open to alternative implementations from someone more familiar with the code base.

## PR Info

In my upgrade efforts from 1.1 to 2.0 I hit the issue of unexpected foreign key violations.  When writing the relations and migrations by hand I was able to skip foreign keys that I did not want, but with `#[sea_orm::model]` I need another way.

This PR adds `#[sea_orm(skip_fk)]` for relation definitions that allow a relation to be created without any foreign key constraints.

## New Features

- [x] Allow `#[sea_orm(skip_fk)]` to skip creating foreign keys

## Example
I am trying to track `movies` that I have available, and a complete list of `watched_movies` including any movies that are currently not available.  This means that there cannot be a foreign key constraint on `watched_movies.id`.
```rust
mod entity {
    pub mod movies {
        use sea_orm::entity::prelude::*;

        #[sea_orm::model]
        #[derive(Debug, Clone, DeriveEntityModel)]
        #[sea_orm(table_name = "movies")]
        pub struct Model {
        #[sea_orm(primary_key)]
        pub id: u32,

        #[sea_orm(has_many, relation_enum = "Watched", from = "id", to = "id")]
        pub watched: HasMany<super::watched_movies::Entity>,
    }

    impl ActiveModelBehavior for ActiveModel {}
}

pub mod watched_movies {
    use sea_orm::entity::prelude::*;

    #[sea_orm::model]
    #[derive(Debug, Clone, DeriveEntityModel)]
    #[sea_orm(table_name = "watched_movies")]
    pub struct Model {
        #[sea_orm(primary_key, auto_increment = false)]
        pub id: u32,
        #[sea_orm(primary_key, auto_increment = false)]
        pub user_id: u32,

        #[sea_orm(belongs_to, relation_enum = "Content", from = "id", to = "id", skip_fk)]
        pub movie: HasOne<super::movies::Entity>,
    }

    impl ActiveModelBehavior for ActiveModel {}
}
```

