Fixes two issues: (1) Vietnamese filenames with diacritics/spaces cause encoding errors in download headers, (2) Docmost exports use `files/` directory instead of `attachments/` for images.

## Changes

**`app/api/download/route.js`**
- Implement RFC 5987 encoding for `Content-Disposition` header with dual format:
  - `filename`: ASCII fallback (non-ASCII → underscore)
  - `filename*`: UTF-8 encoded for proper Unicode support
- Add charset to `Content-Type` header

**`lib/zipUtils.js`**
- Extend `collectAttachments()` to scan both `attachments/` and `files/` directories
- Update `findImageReferences()` to match image paths with either prefix
- Add `files/` prefix fallback in `replaceImageUrls()` search chain
- Include debug warning when filename-only fallback is used

## Example

```javascript
// Before: fails with Vietnamese filenames
'Content-Disposition': `attachment; filename="${filename}"`

// After: supports Unicode with multi-browser compatibility
const encodedFilename = encodeURIComponent(filename)
  .replace(/['()]/g, c => '%' + c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).toUpperCase())
  .replace(/\*/g, '%2A');

'Content-Disposition': `attachment; filename="${filename.replace(/[^\x00-\x7F]/g, '_')}"; filename*=UTF-8''${encodedFilename}`
```

